[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: What a node is

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-NODE-STRUCT
TITLE: What a node is
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
PLACE: screen
STATEMENT: >>>
The types, the fields every node carries, and the two ladders (DEPTH, AUTHORED_BY).

- The grammar is generated from one Nix file. Every node carries a UID, a TITLE, who wrote it (AUTHORED_BY: llm or human) and a STATEMENT; RATIONALE and NOTES are optional, and the parser enforces the field order.
> grammar = values.nix -> generated .sgra; DECISION|MECHANISM|SLICE|INVARIANT|SPIKE; all: UID, TITLE, AUTHORED_BY: llm|human, STATEMENT; RATIONALE, NOTES optional; field order enforced [WORKS]
- A decision (41 on the sheet's day) carries a STATUS of open, accepted, rejected or superseded, and a RETIRES_ON condition. It has no DEPTH and no fingerprint of its own; its only relations are Superseded_By and Backlogged_In.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]
> DECISION (41): +STATUS: open|accepted|rejected|superseded, +RETIRES_ON; no DEPTH, no PARENT_FP; rels Superseded_By, Backlogged_In [WORKS]
- A mechanism (129) carries DEPTH and may carry fingerprints, and can make seven kinds of relation. In the grid it is the descriptive-and-universal cell: how the system always behaves.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM]
> MECHANISM (129): +DEPTH, +PARENT_FP?; rels Governed_By|Guarantees|Serialized_By|Proven_By|Assumes|File|Backlogged_In; grid cell descriptive x universal [WORKS]
- Nobody has asked, node by node, whether each of the 129 mechanisms describes behaviour or imposes a rule. The two that a check enforces are rules — requirements wearing a mechanism prefix.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> MECHANISM per-node mood test ("can it be violated?") over 129 nodes: unwritten; the two check-backed Guarantees sources are normative, not MECHANISM [GAP]
- The grammar's INVARIANT type (8 nodes, same fields as a mechanism) is dissolved: each one is a requirement.
[LINK: DEC-INVARIANT-IS-A-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> INVARIANT (8): fields as MECHANISM; rels Governed_By|Proven_By|Backlogged_In -> REQUIREMENT [FALLS OUT: DEC-INVARIANT-IS-A-REQUIREMENT]
- Two of those eight are not rules at all but measurements — evidence, not requirements. That is exactly the condition under which the ruling says it retires, and nobody has noticed.
[LINK: INV-REGISTER-NOT-AUTHORITATIVE] [LINK: INV-VOCABULARIES-UNBOUNDED] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> INV-REGISTER-NOT-AUTHORITATIVE, INV-VOCABULARIES-UNBOUNDED are measurements (EVIDENCE cell); that trips the ruling's own RETIRES_ON, unnoticed [GAP]
- The grammar's SLICE type (13 nodes, same fields as a mechanism) is dissolved: each one is work that declares what it crosses.
[LINK: DEC-SLICE-DECOMPOSES-TO-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> SLICE (13): fields as MECHANISM; rels Governed_By|Crosses|Assumes|Proven_By|File|Backlogged_In -> WORK carrying Crosses [FALLS OUT: DEC-SLICE-DECOMPOSES-TO-WORK]
- The grammar's SPIKE type (11 nodes) carries two state fields: DEPTH and a STATUS of unrun, run or blocked. It is read as a pipeline — work, then evidence, then the decision it settles — but no decision has ruled that reading.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> SPIKE (11): +STATUS: unrun|run|blocked, +RETIRES_ON beside DEPTH; rels Governed_By|File|Backlogged_In; reading = WORK -> EVIDENCE -> DECISION; no DECISION owns it [GAP]
- Six of the ruled types are absent from the grammar: requirement, use case, evidence, work, narrative and plan (today only a directory). For each, nobody has written which fields it carries, what its state field is, whether it has DEPTH or fingerprints, or whether it may name a file.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-USE-CASE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-NARRATIVE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> REQUIREMENT, USE CASE, EVIDENCE, WORK, NARRATIVE, PLAN: absent from grammar; per type its fields, state field, DEPTH?, PARENT_FP?, may it name a File?: unwritten [GAP]
- A narrative assembles the grid — the claims about the system — for a person to read start to finish, yet the system's stated purpose names a model with a bounded context as the reader. The direction has not reconciled the two.
[LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-NARRATIVE]
> NARRATIVE (assembles the grid for a person, start to finish) vs DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE (reader = a model with bounded context) [UNADDRESSED]
- DEPTH is a six-step ladder — sketch, needs-design, needs-spike, interface-settled, implemented, verified — with one word list shared by every type that carries it. Live counts on the sheet's day: 98, 1, 0, 36, 23, 3.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE]
> DEPTH: sketch|needs-design|needs-spike|interface-settled|implemented|verified; live 98|1|0|36|23|3; one word list shared by the four typed nodes [WORKS]
- Unsaid: whether DEPTH may go back down, where each type's 'settled' line sits, and that 'needs-spike' names a type that no longer exists. STATUS is one field name with two different word lists, and nobody has said whether every state field should be called STATUS.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE]
> DEPTH: may it regress; per-type settled cut; "needs-spike" names a dissolved type. STATUS: one name, two word lists; is every state field called STATUS? [UNADDRESSED]
- AUTHORED_BY is a four-rung ladder — llm, llm-accepted, llm-adopted, human — that only rises. It governs whether an agent may undo a change: freely at the bottom rung, with a report above it, and only by stopping to ask at the top.
> AUTHORED_BY ladder llm < llm-accepted < llm-adopted < human, rises only; governs undo by the writer's class: FREE | SOFT (report the undo) | HARD (stop, ask) [WORKS]
- Who made a write is read off the ssh key: a human or an agent. But the grammar still spells only llm and human, scribe always stamps llm, and there is no move by which a human raises a node up the ladder.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]
> identity:human | identity:llm = the class read off the ssh key that makes a write; ladder spelling: values.nix has llm|human, scribe stamps llm, no raise move [GAP]
- An agent has rewritten 10 of the 31 statements marked human-authored, and the mark did not change. Three remedies were listed — hash the text into the mark, drop the mark, or forbid the edit — and none was chosen; the ladder rules out dropping it.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-IDENTITY-HAS-NO-CARRIER]
> identity:llm rewrote 10 of 31 identity:human STATEMENTs, key unchanged; hash-into-key | drop | forbid unpicked; the ladder forecloses drop [UNADDRESSED]
- A fingerprint is seven hex characters hashed from a parent's contract fields (everything but RATIONALE, NOTES, PARENT_FP and RELATIONS), recorded on the child as PARENT_FP. 0000000 means unsigned. All 31 live entries read 0000000: nothing has ever been signed.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT]
> PARENT_FP: entries UID:hash7 (fingerprint = 7 hex of the parent's contract fields = all but RATIONALE|NOTES|PARENT_FP|RELATIONS); 0000000 = unsigned; 31 live, all 0000000 [UNADDRESSED]
- Which fields count toward a fingerprint, and which fields are reserved to their owner (PARENT_FP and AUTHORED_BY), are hard-coded lists inside scribe. Under the layer stack they are semantic-layer data, and a ruling already says they are configuration.
[LINK: DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE] [LINK: DEC-LAYER-STACK] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-SEMANTICS-LIVE-IN-TOOLS-WITH-NO-OWNER]
> contract-field list and owner-only list (PARENT_FP, AUTHORED_BY) are Python lists inside scribe [FALLS OUT: DEC-LAYER-STACK, L3 (semantic-layer) data; DEC-FP-FIELDS-CONFIGURABLE]
- RETIRES_ON is prose about a future condition, so no joint — a place logic could attach — can evaluate it. And if a probe (grammar: SPIKE) is really work, then evidence, then a decision, its RETIRES_ON has no node to live on.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-EVIDENCE] [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-WORK]
> RETIRES_ON (DECISION, SPIKE): prose, time-based, so unevaluable at a joint (a place logic can attach); SPIKE's copy homeless under the pipeline reading [UNADDRESSED]
- Each plan's backlog is one register node that ungroomed items point at. It asserts nothing about the world, so the grid gives it no cell to sit in.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-MECHANISM]
> backlog register (one MECHANISM node per plan; ungroomed items point at it; holds no list) asserts nothing, so the grid gives it no cell [UNADDRESSED]
- A node's UID is its only stable identity, and the UID's prefix spells its type. So changing a node's type mints a new UID and moves every relation and fingerprint that named the old one.
[LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-GRAMMAR-CANNOT-SPELL-THE-VOCABULARY]
> UID is the only identity (strictdoc's MID is per-parse); the prefix carries type, so a retype re-mints the UID and moves every relation and PARENT_FP entry [GAP]
<<<
